a6b14346e3bb9b0a0dd1ab15e81edab6484ecd77a1dde789df9ef4aa"},"seconds":54.97084665298462,"seed":182222686593251734,"stop":"eos","text":"To find the constants $c_2,$ $c_1,$ and $c_0$ that satisfy the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then equate the coefficients of the corresponding powers of $x$ on both sides of the equation.\n\nFirst, we expand $(x - 3)^3,$ $(x - 3)^2,$ and $(x - 3):$\n\\[(x - 3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27,\\]\n\\[(x - 3)^2 = x^2 - 6x + 9,\\]\n\\[(x - 3) = x - 3.\\]\n\nNow, substituting these into the right-hand side of the given equation, we have:\n\\[(x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2(x^2 - 6x + 9) + c_1(x - 3) + c_0.\\]\n\nExpanding and combining like terms, we get:\n\\[x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2x^2 - 6c_2x + 9c_2 + c_1x - 3c_1 + c_0.\\]\n\nGrouping the terms with the same power of $x,$ we have:\n\\[x^3 + (-9 + c_2)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\\]\n\nWe now equate the coefficients of the corresponding powers of $x$ on both sides of the equation:\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = x^3 + (-9 + c_2)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\\]\n\nFrom this, we get the following system of equations:\n1. Coefficient of $x^3$: $1 = 1$ (this is already satisfied).\n2. Coefficient of $x^2$: $1 = -9 + c_2 \\implies c_2 = 10$.\n3. Coefficient of $x$: $0 = 27 - 6c_2 + c_1 \\implies 0 = 27 - 6(10) + c_1 \\implies 0 = 27 - 60 + c_1 \\implies c_1 = 33$.\n4. Constant term: $-5 = -27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 \\implies -5 = -27 + 9(10) - 3(33) + c_0 \\implies -5 = -27 + 90 - 99 + c_0 \\implies -5 = -36 + c_0 \\implies c_0 = 31$.\n\nNow, we have $c_2 = 10,$ $c_1 = 33,$ and $c_0 = 31$. To find $c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2,$ we calculate:\n\\[c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 33^2 + 31^2 = 100 + 1089 + 961 = 2150.\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{2150}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{2150}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the constants $c_2,$ $c_1,$ and $c_0$ that satisfy the equation\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = (x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0,\\]\nwe will expand the right-hand side and then equate the coefficients of the corresponding powers of $x$ on both sides of the equation.\n\nFirst, we expand $(x - 3)^3,$ $(x - 3)^2,$ and $(x - 3):$\n\\[(x - 3)^3 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27,\\]\n\\[(x - 3)^2 = x^2 - 6x + 9,\\]\n\\[(x - 3) = x - 3.\\]\n\nNow, substituting these into the right-hand side of the given equation, we have:\n\\[(x - 3)^3 + c_2 (x - 3)^2 + c_1 (x - 3) + c_0 = x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2(x^2 - 6x + 9) + c_1(x - 3) + c_0.\\]\n\nExpanding and combining like terms, we get:\n\\[x^3 - 9x^2 + 27x - 27 + c_2x^2 - 6c_2x + 9c_2 + c_1x - 3c_1 + c_0.\\]\n\nGrouping the terms with the same power of $x,$ we have:\n\\[x^3 + (-9 + c_2)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\\]\n\nWe now equate the coefficients of the corresponding powers of $x$ on both sides of the equation:\n\\[x^3 + x^2 - 5 = x^3 + (-9 + c_2)x^2 + (27 - 6c_2 + c_1)x + (-27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0).\\]\n\nFrom this, we get the following system of equations:\n1. Coefficient of $x^3$: $1 = 1$ (this is already satisfied).\n2. Coefficient of $x^2$: $1 = -9 + c_2 \\implies c_2 = 10$.\n3. Coefficient of $x$: $0 = 27 - 6c_2 + c_1 \\implies 0 = 27 - 6(10) + c_1 \\implies 0 = 27 - 60 + c_1 \\implies c_1 = 33$.\n4. Constant term: $-5 = -27 + 9c_2 - 3c_1 + c_0 \\implies -5 = -27 + 9(10) - 3(33) + c_0 \\implies -5 = -27 + 90 - 99 + c_0 \\implies -5 = -36 + c_0 \\implies c_0 = 31$.\n\nNow, we have $c_2 = 10,$ $c_1 = 33,$ and $c_0 = 31$. To find $c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2,$ we calculate:\n\\[c_2^2 + c_1^2 + c_0^2 = 10^2 + 33^2 + 31^2 = 100 + 1089 + 961 = 2150.\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{2150}\\).","id":"intermediate_algebra:417"}
